  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Energy-efficient home building in Texas is mostly about keeping heat out: a tight, well-insulated envelope, windows that block solar gain, a reflective roof, and an air conditioner sized for the house you actually built. Get those right during construction and your home stays more comfortable through long summers while using less electricity. Here is where to focus your budget.</p>

<h2>Why the Texas heat changes the rules</h2>
<p>In much of Texas, cooling dominates home energy use for a large part of the year, and in the Gulf Coast and East Texas, humidity matters as much as temperature. That means two goals: block heat from getting in, and remove moisture efficiently. Many upgrades that make sense in colder climates matter less here, while attic, window and duct choices matter a lot more.</p>
<p>Texas has a statewide residential energy code, and cities adopt and enforce specific editions of the International Energy Conservation Code with local amendments. Treat the code as the floor, and check the current edition with your local permitting office.</p>

<h2>Build a tight, well-insulated envelope</h2>
<h3>Air sealing: the cheapest big win</h3>
<p>Hot, humid air sneaks in through gaps around top plates, penetrations for plumbing and wiring, recessed lights, attic hatches and rim joists. Air sealing those gaps before insulation goes in is one of the most cost-effective things you can do.</p>
<ul>
<li>Ask your builder whether they perform a blower door test, and what result they target.</li>
<li>Seal attic hatches and pull-down stairs with weatherstripping and an insulated cover.</li>
<li>Use gaskets or sealant at electrical boxes on exterior walls.</li>
</ul>
<p>After move-in, check doors and windows yearly. Fresh [[amz:weatherstrip|weatherstripping]] and a [[amz:caulk_gun|good caulk gun]] fix the small leaks that open up over time.</p>

<h3>Insulation and the attic strategy</h3>
<p>In Texas, the attic is the hottest place in the house, and it is often where the ductwork and air handler live. You have two main approaches:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>How it works</th><th>Trade-offs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Vented attic</td><td>Deep blown insulation on the attic floor, often with a radiant barrier under the roof deck</td><td>Typically lower cost; ducts still sit in a hot attic and must be very well sealed and insulated</td></tr>
<tr><td>Unvented (conditioned) attic</td><td>Spray foam or other insulation at the roof deck, bringing the attic inside the thermal envelope</td><td>Often higher cost; ducts and equipment stay in a much cooler space</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Either can perform well when installed carefully. What matters most is continuous coverage with no gaps or compressed spots, and walls insulated to at least code.</p>

<h2>Keep the sun off the house</h2>
<h3>Windows, shading and orientation</h3>
<p>Windows are a major path for summer heat. Look for low-E glass with a low Solar Heat Gain Coefficient (SHGC), which is shown on the window's NFRC label. Then reduce the problem with design:</p>
<ul>
<li>Limit large west-facing windows, which take the harshest afternoon sun.</li>
<li>Use deep roof overhangs and covered porches on the south and west.</li>
<li>Orient the long side of the house east-west where the lot allows.</li>
<li>Plant shade trees on the west side, kept a safe distance from the foundation.</li>
</ul>

<h3>A roof that reflects heat</h3>
<p>Light-colored and "cool roof" rated materials reflect more sunlight and absorb less heat. Metal roofing with reflective finishes is popular in Texas, and lighter shingles also help. Pair the roof with a radiant barrier or a conditioned attic, and make sure attic ventilation is designed correctly for whichever approach you chose.</p>

<h2>Right-sized HVAC and sealed ducts</h2>
<p>Bigger is not better. An oversized air conditioner cools quickly, shuts off, and leaves the house clammy because it never runs long enough to remove humidity. Insist on:</p>
<ol>
<li>A Manual J load calculation for your actual plans</li>
<li>Duct design (Manual D) and sealed, insulated ductwork</li>
<li>A high-efficiency system, and consider a variable-speed unit for better humidity control</li>
<li>A duct leakage test if your jurisdiction or builder offers one</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Ask your HVAC contractor for a copy of the load calculation and check that it uses your real window specs, insulation levels and orientation. A calculation built on generic defaults is a red flag for an oversized system.</p></div>

<h2>Smart controls, solar and future-ready wiring</h2>
<h3>Controls, lighting and monitoring</h3>
<p>Once the envelope is solid, smart controls squeeze out more savings. A smart thermostat learns your schedule and lets you adjust from your phone, which helps on hot afternoons when nobody is home. LED lighting throughout produces far less heat than old bulbs, so your AC works less too.</p>
<p>During the final walkthrough and the first summer, an [[amz:ir_thermometer|infrared thermometer]] helps you spot hot walls, poorly insulated spots and leaky supply registers.</p>
<!--products:smart_thermostat,led_bulbs,ir_thermometer,thermal_camera|Gear to control and check your home's energy use-->

<h3>Solar and future-ready wiring</h3>
<p>Solar panels can make sense in sunny Texas, but they work best on a home that is already efficient. Even if you are not adding solar now, ask about roof orientation, conduit from the roof to the electrical panel, and panel capacity for future solar, batteries or an EV charger. Our post on <a href="/smart-home-features-to-add-during-new-construction/">smart home features to add during new construction</a> covers the wiring to plan for.</p>

<h2>Verify the work before drywall</h2>
<p>Efficiency features only pay off if they are installed correctly, and most of them disappear behind drywall. Walk the house at the pre-drywall stage and look for insulation gaps, unsealed penetrations, crushed or disconnected ducts, and missing baffles at the eaves. Take photos of every wall and ceiling so you have a record of what is behind the finishes. If you are owner-building, consider hiring an energy rater or third-party inspector for this step; they can run blower door and duct leakage tests and flag problems while they are still cheap to fix.</p>

<h2>Next steps</h2>
<p>Efficiency upgrades should be in your budget from the start; our <a href="/budgeting-for-your-texas-home-build-tips-for-staying-on-track/">Texas build budgeting guide</a> shows how to prioritize them. Use the <a href="/new-construction-inspection-checklist/">new construction inspection checklist</a> to verify insulation and air sealing before drywall goes up, and take the <a href="/free-training/">free training</a> to plan your build from the ground up.</p>
